.TH CLRU_SET 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_set \- store a value by key (a type-inferred convenience wrapper)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clru_set(name, key, val);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
A type-inferred GNU C statement expression that converts key and val
to the declared KeyT and ValT of the cache, in the same way as a plain
C assignment, serialises the converted values into cmap_pair values,
and calls clrucache_set_full().

The macro converts val to ValT instead of storing the natural
expression type of val unchanged, so an int literal that goes into a
double-valued cache stores the double value 5.0, not a 4-byte copy of
the bit pattern of the int. The compiler must be able to convert a val
or key expression implicitly to the declared ValT or KeyT of the
cache; one that it cannot convert is a compile error at this point. A
character-pointer KeyT or ValT is the exception: the macro keeps the
pointer type of the expression, so a const char * key or value for a
char * cache needs no cast.

A key of a struct type is the same key as another one only when every
byte of the two is equal, padding included; see KEYS WITH PADDING in
clrucache_create_full(3). With GCC 11 or later this macro sets the
padding of its own copy of the key to zero.

If the caller configured a remote setter at construction, the library
calls it synchronously before it updates the cache. When the setter
fails, the macro returns ccol_unexpected_failure and the cache does not
change for that key; even the position of that key in the eviction
order stays the same. While the setter runs, a concurrent insert that
needs room can evict the key like any other entry; see
clrucache_set_full(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Cache variable. It must be a plain variable, declared with
      clru_construct or clru_declare, or restored with
      clru_redeclare() in the current scope, because that is what
      gives the KeyT and the ValT for the conversion above. name
      cannot be any other expression, for example a read of a struct
      member.
key   Key expression: any expression that the compiler can convert
      implicitly to KeyT.
val   Value expression: any expression that the compiler can convert
      implicitly to ValT.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success             The macro stored the value, and the remote
                         setter succeeded, if there is one.
ccol_unexpected_failure  The remote setter returned false. The set
                         changed nothing, but a concurrent insert can
                         have evicted the key (see above).
ccol_invalid_args        cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, or cache is a
                         stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                         an argument is a null pointer, or an argument
                         has a size of zero. A key or a value of a
                         character-pointer type that is NULL is not a
                         string, so it gives this code.
ccol_not_enough_memory   The allocation of memory for the new value
                         failed.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
This macro uses a GNU C statement expression ({...}), so it needs GCC
or Clang.
When you pass the cache handle across scopes, for example into a
thread function, declare a local alias and call clru_redeclare() on it
before you call clru_set(); clru_get() needs the same. See
clru_redeclare(3).
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clru_construct(cache, int, double, 128, NULL, NULL, NULL);

int k = 42;
double v = 3.14;
clru_set(cache, k, v);

clru_destroy(cache);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_get (3),
.BR clrucache_set_full (3)
